course: religion-ethics
lesson: 96
title: Honest money: earning, raising and paying fairly
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Honest money means earning it by doing the work you promised, paying the real price, and giving all of money raised for a cause to that cause.
explain: Leah gets £1 for walking the neighbour's dog round the park, but only goes round the block and says, "Yeah, did the full park"; pay is a promise both ways, so she should do the full walk or say what she really did.
explain: The Bible tells of people who were near good people but still dishonest with money: Gehazi, Elisha's servant (2 Kings 5:20-27), and Judas, who kept the disciples' money bag (John 12:6).
explain: Lies about money tend to grow, because each needs another to protect it, and "nobody's watching" is exactly when honesty counts, like at a farm stall with an honesty box, a box where you put the money yourself because the farmer trusts people to pay, and most do.
explain: If a grown-up asks you to fib to save money, you can gently say the truth, like "Dad, I'm 12 though", without being rude.
fact: "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart, as working for the Lord" (Colossians 3:23); pay is a promise both ways.
fact: The vicar says: "We honour our parents best when we help them keep faith with the truth." "We must obey God rather than human beings" (Acts 5:29).
fact: Child prices are an agreement between the cinema and its customers; saying a false age breaks the deal.
fact: "You shall not steal" and "You shall not bear false witness" (Exodus 20:15-16): pretending you didn't know you spent real money breaks both; telling the truth lets a parent ask the shop for a refund and keeps their trust.
fact: Elisha, a prophet, had healed Naaman, a foreign army commander, and refused his gifts; Gehazi ran after Naaman, lied to get silver and clothes, then told Elisha, "Your servant didn't go anywhere" (2 Kings 5:20-27); Elisha knew, and Gehazi was struck with Naaman's skin disease.
fact: Judas kept the disciples' money bag and "used to help himself to what was put into it" (John 12:6); the Bible tells the truth about people, even the disciples.
fact: Money raised for a cause must go to that cause unless everyone agreed otherwise; Paul handled collected gifts openly (2 Corinthians 8:20-21).
fact: Swapping price stickers is stealing: "The Lord detests differing weights, and dishonest scales do not please him" (Proverbs 20:23).
fact: Money found in a second-hand book probably belonged to the person who gave it, or now to the charity; "Do the same if you find... anything else they have lost" (Deuteronomy 22:3).
fact: A charity sign must be accurate because people pay more because of it; Ananias and Sapphira gave part but pretended it was all (Acts 5:1-11).
fact: Taxes pay for schools, hospitals and roads; Jesus said, "Give back to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's" (Mark 12:17), and Paul, "If you owe taxes, pay taxes" (Romans 13:7).
fact: At Newcastle University in 2006, people paid much more into an honesty box for drinks when a picture of eyes hung above it: we are tempted most when we feel unseen.
fact: God sees what is done in secret (Psalm 139:1-4); "Let no debt remain outstanding, except the continuing debt to love one another" (Romans 13:8).
fact: Slang check: "nicked" means stole; "lol" means laughing out loud; "gurt" (West Country) means very or big.
exercise: Leah only walked the dog round the block but says "did the full park". What should she do? | Do the full walk, or say honestly what she did | Keep the £1, a walk is a walk | Ask for £2 because she was tired out from all that walking | Tell the neighbour the dog didn't want to go
exercise: At the cinema Dad whispers, "Just say you're 11, it's cheaper." Olivia is 12. What is a good response? | Quietly say, "Dad, I'm 12 though" | Say she's 11, because Dad asked | Say she's 10 to save even more | Refuse to go to the cinema at all
exercise: Jayden spent £30 on game coins and said he didn't know it was real money. He did. What should he do? | Tell Mum the truth; she may get a refund and trust him more | Keep saying he didn't know, so that he won't get into any trouble | Spend more so the first £30 isn't noticed | Blame his little sister for using the tablet
exercise: What did Gehazi tell Elisha after running after Naaman? | Your servant didn't go anywhere | I took the silver for the poor | Naaman gave it to me as a present | I am sorry, I have sinned
exercise: What happened to Gehazi? | He was struck with Naaman's skin disease | He became rich and famous, and lived in a palace | He became a great prophet, just like Elisha | Nothing; Elisha never found out what he did
exercise: What does John 12:6 say Judas did with the money bag? | He used to help himself to what was put into it | He gave it all away to the poor, every single day | He lost it in the river when crossing to Jerusalem | He gave it to Peter to look after because he trusted him
exercise: Why does the Bible tell us about a thief among the disciples? | It tells the truth about people and hides neither faults nor mercy | Because all disciples were thieves | Because stealing was allowed back then | It doesn't; Judas never took anything
exercise: Hannah wants to keep £1 each from the cake stall. What should they do? | Hand it all over, and ask the organiser if helpers get anything | Keep £1 each, they worked hard | Keep £2 each and tell the organiser they sold less than they did | Spend it on cakes for themselves
exercise: Ryan's friend swaps price stickers on a Lego set. "Literally their fault lol." What is it really? | Stealing; put it back and don't help | Clever shopping that saves money for his family | Fine, because the shop is big and won't notice | The shop's fault for using stickers that peel off
exercise: Zoe finds £10 in a second-hand book from the charity shop. Whose is it? | Take it back; it's the donor's or the charity's | It's Zoe's, because she bought the book and everything in it | It's the bookshop's next door | It belongs to whoever wrote the book
exercise: Sienna's sign says "For the animal shelter!" but she'll keep half. What is honest? | Change the sign to "Half goes to the shelter", or give it all | Keep the sign; some of the money still goes to the shelter, after all | Keep all the money; nobody checks | Take the sign down and keep everything
exercise: Grandad pays a builder in cash, "No receipt, it's cheaper." What do taxes pay for? | Schools, hospitals and roads | Only the King's palaces and parties | Nothing useful at all, it's just wasted | The builder's holidays and new van
exercise: The farm stall has an honesty box. "Nobody's watching, gurt easy," says a lad. What does "gurt" mean? | Very or big | Not very much | Quite | Never
exercise: What did the 2006 Newcastle honesty box study find? | People paid more when a picture of eyes hung above the box | People paid more when nobody at all was in the room to see them | The pictures made no difference at all | People stopped buying drinks completely
exercise: What does Mark 12:17 say? | Give back to Caesar what is Caesar's and to God what is God's | Never pay anything to Caesar, because all money belongs to God | Give everything you own to Caesar | Caesar must pay taxes to God
exercise: Why should Sienna's sign be accurate? | People pay more because of a charity sign | Signs must always be written in capitals | Animal shelters check every lemonade stand | Lemonade costs more for charity
test: Your mate says, "Lying about your age at the cinema isn't stealing, it's saving." What is accurate? | It breaks the deal with the cinema and takes what you didn't pay for | He's right; saving money is never wrong | It only counts if they check your ID | Cinemas expect people to lie about age
test: "Mum'll kill me, I'll just say I didn't know it was real money," says Jayden. What is the honest move? | Tell the truth; she may get a refund and trust him more | Say his friend did it on her tablet when he wasn't looking | Hide the tablet so she can't check | Delete the game and say nothing
test: Ollie asks, "Even Jesus's mates nicked stuff?" What is the best answer? | Judas did; the Bible is honest about faults, and being near good people doesn't make you honest | No, the Bible says none of the disciples ever did wrong | Yes, all twelve disciples stole regularly | Judas only borrowed it and gave it back
test: A cartoon shows a kid who swaps price stickers and is called a "smart shopper". What is wrong? | Swapping stickers is stealing, not smart shopping | Nothing; that is smart shopping, and everybody does it | He should have swapped even more stickers while he was there | Smart shoppers always pay the full price for everything
test: Hannah says, "We baked for hours, we deserve £1 each." What is the fair approach? | Hand it all over and ask the organiser if helpers get anything | Take £1 each, nobody will notice | Take £5 each, because they baked loads and deserve a proper reward | Keep it all and give the cakes away free
test: What would happen if lemonade stands kept half and still said "For the shelter"? | People would stop trusting charity signs, and real charities would lose out | Shelters would get more money than ever | Nothing; nobody reads signs anyway | Lemonade would become free everywhere
test: Grandad says, "Everyone pays builders cash, no receipt, that's life." What can a child fairly do? | Ask him about it kindly; taxes pay for schools and hospitals | Report Grandad to the police at once | Shout at Grandad until he pays the tax | Nothing; it's always fine if everyone does it
test: "Nobody's watching, take the eggs, lad," says a boy at the farm stall. Why do honesty boxes work at all? | The farmer trusts people to pay, and most do | Because the farmer hides behind the hedge to watch | Because eggs are too cheap for anyone to steal | They don't work; everyone takes the eggs for free
test: Zoe's friend says, "You bought the book, so the £10 is a bonus." What is the honest choice? | Take it back to the charity shop | Keep it, because she paid for the book | Spend half and return half | Put it back in the book and sell the book again
test: Gehazi's lie needed another lie. What does this show about money lies? | Lies tend to grow, so stopping at the first is easiest | One lie is always enough to get away with it, if you're careful | Lies about money never need more lies | Elisha should have kept quiet about it
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the obligation of a person who accepts payment for a task? | The performance of the task as agreed, or a truthful account of what was performed | The retention of payment irrespective of performance | The performance of a lesser task without disclosure of the difference to the person paying | The demand of additional payment for partial performance
exam: Select the statement consistent with the account recorded in 2 Kings 5:20-27. | Gehazi obtained silver and clothing by deceit and subsequently lied to Elisha concerning his absence | Gehazi declined all gifts offered by Naaman | Elisha instructed Gehazi to pursue Naaman for payment | Naaman obtained silver from Gehazi by deceit
exam: Identify the principle governing money raised publicly for a charitable cause. | Such money ought to be applied wholly to that cause unless all parties have agreed otherwise | Such money may be retained by those who collected it | Such money may be divided among the organisers in equal shares | Such money belongs to whoever donated the largest sum
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the 2006 study of an honesty box at Newcastle University is consistent with the established facts. | Contributions increased considerably when an image of eyes was displayed above the box | Contributions ceased entirely when an image of eyes was displayed | Contributions were unaffected by any image displayed | Contributions increased only when no image whatsoever was displayed
